// A range's counter is a binding, and a `const` is in scope in every function, so the counter
// may not take a const's name. The same rule `let`, a parameter, a `match` arm and `for x in xs`
// all follow.
//
// **This fixture exists because the check was missing and nothing noticed.** `const` (A2) added
// `shadows_a_const` to the four places a name could enter scope; ranges (A6) added a FIFTH and
// was written against a tree where `const` did not exist yet. The result was `for N in xs`
// refused and `for N in 0..3` accepted — by the same compiler, one line apart — while the whole
// suite stayed green, because no pass fixture happens to write both a `const` and a range over
// the same name. Stage-1 was accidentally right, having copied its range arm from its array arm.
//
// A pass fixture cannot tell "supported" from "not examined", and neither can a green suite tell
// "consistent" from "never combined". Found by running the two features together on purpose.
const LIMIT: Int = 4;

for LIMIT in 0..3 {
    print(LIMIT);
}
